### Initial conditions

Small LCDM cosmological simulation generated by Chris Power. Cosmology
is WMAP9 and the box is 100Mpc/h in size with 64^3 particles.
We use a softening length of 1/25th of the mean inter-particle separation.

The ICs have been generated to run with Gadget-2 so we need to switch
on the options to cancel the h-factors and a-factors at reading time.

This example runs SWIFT in dark-matter-only mode with inline FOF group
finding enabled. The resulting snapshots are then analysed with HBT-HERONS
to produce subhalo catalogues and merger trees.

MD5 checksum of the ICs:
08736c3101fd738e22f5159f78e6022b  small_cosmo_volume.hdf5

### Compiling SWIFT

SWIFT requires FOF support to be enabled at configure time (./configure --enable-fof)

### Compiling HBT-HERONS

HBT-HERONS requires a C++ compiler with OpenMP support, CMake (version 3.12 or
higher), HDF5 and MPI. Full installation instructions are at
https://hbt-herons.strw.leidenuniv.nl/installation/

    git clone https://github.com/SWIFTSIM/HBT-HERONS.git && cd HBT-HERONS
    mkdir build && cd build
    cmake ../ -DHBT_DM_ONLY=On && make -j 4

The HBT_DM_ONLY flag is set because this is a dark-matter-only simulation. Note we
compile from a separate build directory, as HBT-HERONS records whether its tracked
files have been modified. This produces the executable build/HBT, which is what
run.sh needs to be pointed at.

### How to run

Before running, set hbt_path at the top of run.sh to the HBT executable built above.
The remaining options at the top of run.sh control how many processes are used. They
have working defaults, so only change them if you want to:

    num_swift_threads   (default 8)   threads used by SWIFT
    num_mpi_ranks       (default 4)   MPI ranks used by HBT-HERONS
    num_omp_threads     (default 2)   OpenMP threads per HBT-HERONS rank

Then:

    ./run.sh

This will:
1. Download the initial conditions (if not already present).
2. Run SWIFT with self-gravity and inline FOF, writing 59 snapshots from z=9 to
   z=0 into ./outputs/SWIFT/. Note we require a large number of snapshots to
   produce reliable results as HBT-HERONS is a history-based subhalo finder.
3. Run HBT-HERONS to produce subhalo catalogues in ./outputs/HBT-HERONS/.
4. Plot the z=0 halo mass function and save it as HMF.png.

### Plotting script

plotHMF.py shows a minimal example of loading an HBT-HERONS catalogue. More detailed
example scripts covering merger trees, satellite populations and population-level
statistics can be found at https://hbt-herons.strw.leidenuniv.nl/examples/overview/
